vocabulary). Classification uses sequence classification header, The layer maps from 768 hidden units to only 2 units, where the 2 units represent the two classes id.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic of a GPT-style transformer model architecture and task-specific output heads.\n\nVisible content:\n- Left panel: a vertical “GPT model” pipeline from “Inputs” to “Outputs.”\n- Input stages labeled:\n  - Tokenized text\n  - Token embedding layer\n  - Positional embedding layer\n  - Dropout\n- Main repeated transformer block marked “12×,” containing:\n  - LayerNorm 1\n  - Masked Multi-Head Attention\n  - Dropout\n  - Residual/addition node\n  - LayerNorm 2\n  - Feed Forward\n  - Dropout\n  - Residual/addition node\n- Final stages:\n  - Final LayerNorm\n  - Linear output layer\n- Right side: three output head diagrams connected by dashed arrows:\n  - “Causal Language Header For Pretrain,” showing a dense mapping from hidden size 768 to vocabulary/output size 50257.\n  - “Sequence Classification Header For Classification tuning,” showing mapping from hidden size 768 to 2 classes.\n  - “Causal Language Header For Instruction tuning,” again showing mapping from hidden size 768 to output size 50257.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- None visible.\n\nModel interfaces / transformations:\n- Text tokens are transformed through token and positional embeddings, dropout, repeated transformer blocks with masked self-attention and feed-forward layers, final normalization, and a linear output layer.\n- The architecture branches conceptually to different heads for pretraining, sequence classification fine-tuning, and instruction tuning.\n\nFindings:\n- The figure illustrates that the same GPT backbone can connect to different task heads: causal language modeling for pretraining/instruction tuning and sequence classification for classification tuning."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 10, "caption": "Figure 3. On the left, Figure A shows the word vectors generated by the pre-trained model. Blue points represent DNA word vectors, and red points represent English word vectors. On the right, Figure B shows the word vectors generated by the fine-tuned model. Again, blue points represent DNA word vectors, and red points represent English word vectors.In Figure A, it is evident that the DNA word vectors and English word vectors are generally far apart, with a clear boundary between them. In contrast, in Figure B, the DNA word vectors and English word vectors are noticeably closer, with some overlapping regions. This further illustrates the reason for language capability transfer in the fine-tuned model.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nTwo-panel scientific plot showing PCA projections of vector embeddings.\n\nPanel A:\n- Labeled “A” and “Pretrained model”.\n- Title: “PCA of DNA and English Word Vectors”.\n- Scatter plot with axes “Principal Component 1” and “Principal Component 2”.\n- Legend indicates blue points are “DNA Vectors” and red points are “English Vectors”.\n- DNA vectors form a compact blue cluster on the left, mostly separated from the red English vector cloud on the right.\n- English vectors form a larger red cluster with broader vertical spread.\n\nPanel B:\n- Labeled “B” and “Finetune model”.\n- Same title, axes, and legend.\n- Blue DNA vectors appear as a curved/elongated cluster on the left.\n- Red English vectors form a broad curved distribution spanning the center to right.\n- Compared with Panel A, the two vector spaces appear closer and more continuous after finetuning, with some overlap/transition between DNA and English vectors.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- DNA sequence-derived vectors are shown, but no biological specimen, cell, tissue, organism, or molecular structure is directly imaged.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- High-dimensional DNA and English word embeddings are reduced using PCA into two principal components.\n- The figure compares embedding geometry for a pretrained model versus a finetuned model."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Figure 4. Structure of the Genome Catalog. Each genome is organized by chromosome, forming the highest-level directory. We divided the chromosome sequences into equal-sized segments, referred to as 'Parts.' For each part, we further subdivided into 'Chapters' and 'Sections,' where each section represents specific DNA sequence paragraphs.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic hierarchy diagram using a book analogy for genome organization.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Top-level label: “Genome Book” in a dark blue rounded rectangle.\n- Branching hierarchy below it:\n  - “Chromosome 1”\n    - “ChromosomePart1”\n      - “DNA Chapter1”\n        - “DNA Section1”\n          - “DNA para 1”\n          - “DNA para 2”\n        - “DNASection2”\n      - “DNA Chapter2”\n    - “ChromosomePart2”\n  - “Chromosome 2”\n  - “......”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Genome\n- Chromosomes\n- DNA subdivisions represented metaphorically as parts, chapters, sections, and paragraphs.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No experimental transformation, computational model, or model interface is shown.\n- The figure is a conceptual mapping of genome structure to a book-like hierarchy.\n\nFindings:\n- The figure conveys that a genome can be organized hierarchically into chromosomes and smaller DNA units, analogous to a book divided into chapters, sections, and paragraphs."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 5. Screenshot of the Genomic Book Content, where Figure A on the left shows the DNA sequence content, and Figure B on the right shows the translated English content.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image shows a two-panel figure labeled **A** and **B**. Both panels appear to be screenshots of document/PDF pages with a left sidebar of page thumbnails and a main page view.\n\nPanel **A** shows a page containing DNA-like nucleotide sequence text, including headings such as **“CHAPTER-2”**, **“SECTION-1”**, and repeated bases such as A, C, G, and T arranged in lines.\n\nPanel **B** shows another document page with mixed, partly distorted text. It includes visible headings such as **“CHAPTER-2”** and **“SECTION-1”**, with words resembling biological/sequence-related labels, but the text is degraded and not fully legible.\n\nVisible biological source objects: nucleotide/DNA sequence text in panel A.  \nVisible transformations/model interfaces: document viewer/PDF interface screenshots; panel B appears to show corrupted or transformed text relative to normal readable sequence/document text.  \nFindings: the figure visually compares readable biological sequence/document content with a degraded or altered text output, but the exact method or conclusion cannot be determined from the image alone."]}]

